Previously, Container Security responsibilities were shared across a small team, with specific platforms and workstreams owned by different team members or escalated to more senior technical leadership when broader architectural decisions were required. As the team lost capacity and the Container Security ecosystem continued to grow, several important ownership and capability gaps became more visible.
Assuming SVP-level technical duties. Following Chris C.’s departure, there was a gap in senior technical ownership for architectural and design decisions. Previously, implementation patterns such as the VSaaS Gating design were reviewed with Chris before moving forward. Rimas has since taken on this responsibility, reviewing proposed solutions and providing technical direction on Gating, DiP, VSaaS changes and VSaaS-LSE integrations. His redesign of the Gating solution is also addressing a specific CAP/Risk Exception.
Taking ownership of FITR. Following Houssem’s departure, Rimas assumed primary ownership of FITR and FITR Reporting. He took over an area that required continued delivery and operational support, including guiding the pyartifactory migration to ensure FITR data remained available. FITR Reporting is now operational and maintained within the team.
Taking on new technical leadership responsibilities. CHRIS and the Operator/Platform image ingestion work did not have an existing technical owner or established architecture. Rimas took responsibility for defining the technical approach and driving both initiatives from the initial design through implementation. CHRIS progressed from a proof of concept to an MVP within one month with a two-engineer team, while the Operator/Platform ingestion pipeline has already executed approximately 1,000 times during the migration.
Identifying and addressing gaps outside his assigned products. In addition to these responsibilities, Rimas independently identified the lack of a standalone malware detection and warning capability outside VSaaS and 3PiiP and built one himself. The capability has already detected two malware events before they reached SOC detection or the production environment.
